The two pointers walk through both lists. When one reaches the end, it starts from the other list's head. This makes both pointers travel the same total distance, so they meet at the shared node or at `nullptr`.

The comparison must use node addresses, not node values. Two different nodes can store the same value.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode *getIntersectionNode(ListNode *headA, ListNode *headB) {
        ListNode *pointerA = headA;
        ListNode *pointerB = headB;

        while (pointerA != pointerB) {
            pointerA = (pointerA == NULL) ? headB : pointerA->next;
            pointerB = (pointerB == NULL) ? headA : pointerB->next;
        }

        return pointerA;
    }
};
```

For example, with `A: 1 -> 2 -> 7` and `B: 4 -> 5 -> 7`, after switching heads both pointers reach node `7` together. The lists may also be completely separate, in which case the loop ends with `nullptr`.

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Both lists are pushed into arrays first. The last nodes are compared while moving backward. Shared linked lists have the same tail, so the last equal node found during this backward scan is the intersection point.

The stack arrays make this method use extra memory, but it avoids changing the lists and makes the common-tail idea visible in the code.

```javascript
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    constructor(val = 0, next = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    getIntersectionNode(headA, headB) {
        const stackA = [];
        const stackB = [];

        let current = headA;
        while (current !== null) {
            stackA.push(current);
            current = current.next;
        }

        current = headB;
        while (current !== null) {
            stackB.push(current);
            current = current.next;
        }

        let intersection = null;
        while (stackA.length > 0 && stackB.length > 0) {
            const nodeA = stackA[stackA.length - 1];
            const nodeB = stackB[stackB.length - 1];

            if (nodeA !== nodeB) {
                break;
            }

            intersection = nodeA;
            stackA.pop();
            stackB.pop();
        }

        return intersection;
    }
}
```

Dry run: if both stacks end with `common`, that node is saved. The scan continues until the nodes differ, leaving `common` as the answer. When the lists do not share a tail, the first comparison already differs and `null` is returned.

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`.
Space complexity: `O(n + m)` for the two stacks.

This is the direct comparison method. Each node in the first list is compared with every node in the second list. Since linked list nodes are compared as pointers, a match means both paths reached the exact same object.

It is slower than the pointer methods, but the code is easy to follow and does not change either input list.

```go
/*
Definition for singly-linked list.
type ListNode struct {
    Val  int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func getIntersectionNode(headA, headB *ListNode) *ListNode {
    for first := headA; first != nil; first = first.Next {
        for second := headB; second != nil; second = second.Next {
            if first == second {
                return first
            }
        }
    }

    return nil
}
```

The no-intersection case returns `nil` after all pairs have been checked. Empty input lists also work because the outer or inner loop is skipped.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Here the lengths of the two lists are found first. The longer list is moved ahead by the length difference. After that, both pointers have the same number of nodes left, so they can be compared one step at a time.

A shared node is detected with `first == second`. Checking `val` would be wrong because equal values do not mean the nodes are the same.

```java
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;

    ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
    }

    ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
}
 */

class Solution {
    public ListNode getIntersectionNode(ListNode headA, ListNode headB) {
        int lengthA = getLength(headA);
        int lengthB = getLength(headB);

        ListNode first = headA;
        ListNode second = headB;

        while (lengthA > lengthB) {
            first = first.next;
            lengthA--;
        }

        while (lengthB > lengthA) {
            second = second.next;
            lengthB--;
        }

        while (first != second) {
            first = first.next;
            second = second.next;
        }

        return first;
    }

    private int getLength(ListNode node) {
        int length = 0;
        while (node != null) {
            length++;
            node = node.next;
        }
        return length;
    }
}
```

An empty list is handled naturally because its length is zero and its pointer is `null`. If there is no shared node, both pointers become `null` at the same time.

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

